      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "BLK_GU_JEEMAIN2027_MAT_9BB8FB46_CONCEPTS_01_DEF_AND_NOTATION",
          "semanticAnchorId": "ANCHOR_SET_DEFINITION_AND_MEMBERSHIP",
          "title": "1. Set Definition and Element Membership",
          "contentMarkdown": "A **set** is a well-defined collection of distinct objects. If an object $a$ is an element of set $A$, we write $a \\in A$. If $b$ is not an element of $A$, we write $b \\notin A$.",
          "boundAtomicFactIds": [
            "AF_GU_JEEMAIN2027_MAT_9BB8FB46_D1B6F61D",
            "AF_GU_JEEMAIN2027_MAT_9BB8FB46_8E8CCB25"
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "BLK_GU_JEEMAIN2027_MAT_9BB8FB46_CONCEPTS_02_REPRESENTATIONS_AND_NUMBER_SETS",
          "semanticAnchorId": "ANCHOR_REPRESENTATIONS_AND_NUMBER_SETS",
          "title": "2. Representations and Standard Number Sets",
          "contentMarkdown": "Sets are standardly represented in two forms:\n1. **Roster / Tabular Form**: Elements are listed separated by commas within braces $\\{\\}$, where order is immaterial and elements are not repeated.\n2. **Set-Builder Form**: Elements are described by their characterizing property: $A = \\{x : P(x)\\}$.\n\nStandard number sets: $\\mathbb{N}$ (natural numbers), $\\mathbb{Z}$ (integers), $\\mathbb{Q} = \\{p/q : p, q \\in \\mathbb{Z}, q \\neq 0\\}$ (rationals), and $\\mathbb{R}$ (reals).",
          "boundAtomicFactIds": [
            "AF_GU_JEEMAIN2027_MAT_9BB8FB46_621F66AA",
            "AF_GU_JEEMAIN2027_MAT_9BB8FB46_D3AE90CF",
            "AF_GU_JEEMAIN2027_MAT_9BB8FB46_6FF4E366"
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "BLK_GU_JEEMAIN2027_MAT_9BB8FB46_CONCEPTS_03_EMPTY_FINITE_EQUALITY",
          "semanticAnchorId": "ANCHOR_EMPTY_FINITE_EQUALITY",
          "title": "3. Empty Set, Finite/Infinite Sets, and Set Equality",
          "contentMarkdown": "The **empty set** (null/void set) contains no elements, denoted by $\\phi$ or $\\{\\}$, with cardinality $n(\\phi) = 0$.\nA set is **finite** if it consists of a definite number of distinct elements $n(A)$; otherwise it is **infinite**.\nTwo sets $A$ and $B$ are **equal** ($A = B$) if they have identical elements, whereas sets with $n(A) = n(B)$ are **equivalent**.",
          "boundAtomicFactIds": [
            "AF_GU_JEEMAIN2027_MAT_9BB8FB46_E7B2ED50",
            "AF_GU_JEEMAIN2027_MAT_9BB8FB46_2E310406",
            "AF_GU_JEEMAIN2027_MAT_9BB8FB46_87E2CF75"
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "BLK_GU_JEEMAIN2027_MAT_9BB8FB46_CONCEPTS_04_SUBSETS_AND_INTERVALS",
          "semanticAnchorId": "ANCHOR_SUBSETS_AND_INTERVALS",
          "title": "4. Subsets and Intervals of Real Numbers",
          "contentMarkdown": "$A$ is a **subset** of $B$ ($A \\subseteq B$) if every element of $A$ is in $B$. If $A \\subseteq B$ and $A \\neq B$, $A$ is a **proper subset** ($A \\subset B$).\nAxioms: Every set is a subset of itself ($A \\subseteq A$) and $\\phi \\subseteq A$ for all $A$. Also, $A = B \\iff A \\subseteq B \\land B \\subseteq A$.\n\nSubsets of $\\mathbb{R}$ as intervals:\n- Open interval: $(a, b) = \\{x \\in \\mathbb{R} : a < x < b\\}$\n- Closed interval: $[a, b] = \\{x \\in \\mathbb{R} : a \\le x \\le b\\}$\n- Semi-open intervals: $[a, b) = \\{x \\in \\mathbb{R} : a \\le x < b\\}$ and $(a, b] = \\{x \\in \\mathbb{R} : a < x \\le b\\}$.\n- Interval length is $(b - a)$.",
          "boundAtomicFactIds": [
            "AF_GU_JEEMAIN2027_MAT_9BB8FB46_8092F0EE",
            "AF_GU_JEEMAIN2027_MAT_9BB8FB46_CC297DF5",
            "AF_GU_JEEMAIN2027_MAT_9BB8FB46_871D5B7E",
            "AF_GU_JEEMAIN2027_MAT_9BB8FB46_79B21169",
            "AF_GU_JEEMAIN2027_MAT_9BB8FB46_9C2209FD"
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "BLK_GU_JEEMAIN2027_MAT_9BB8FB46_CONCEPTS_05_POWER_SET_AND_UNIVERSAL_SET",
          "semanticAnchorId": "ANCHOR_POWER_SET_AND_UNIVERSAL_SET",
          "title": "5. Power Set and Universal Set",
          "contentMarkdown": "The **power set** $P(A)$ is the collection of all subsets of $A$, with $\\phi \\in P(A)$ and $A \\in P(A)$.\nIf $n(A) = m$, then $n(P(A)) = 2^m$, and the number of proper subsets is $2^m - 1$.\nA **universal set** $U$ is the comprehensive superset containing all objects and sets under consideration in a given context.",
          "boundAtomicFactIds": [
            "AF_GU_JEEMAIN2027_MAT_9BB8FB46_69C8729D",
            "AF_GU_JEEMAIN2027_MAT_9BB8FB46_39AE4B48",
            "AF_GU_JEEMAIN2027_MAT_9BB8FB46_72B3D5FA"
          ]
        }
      ],
